{
 "note": "A real PEDAGOGICAL PIECE, not a scale table \u2014 and the only fixture here that was DOUBLE-KEYED and arbitrated. Use it to measure the arranger on the kind of music the app actually teaches.",
 "source": "F. A. Kummer, Violoncellschule Op.60, ed. Hugo Becker (C. F. Peters), printed p.50, Nachtrag '101 Uebungsstuecke' no.9 \u2014 A major, 3/4, a duet of two braced bass-clef staves, 24 bars. Both staves are fingered.",
 "licence": "PD. Kummer d.1879; Becker d.1941 -> PD since 2012.",
 "provenance": "Read TWICE independently (the second reader blind, writing to separate paths). The readings agreed on 47/47 bar note-counts, 95/101 pitches and 65/66 digits; where BOTH claimed high confidence they agreed 81/81. The 7 disagreements were settled by an arbiter working from the native plate with non-metric arguments (a printed 0 can only be an open string; a ledger line through a head is categorical; a finger no string can place refutes the reading). Outcome: reader A 3, reader B 2, NEITHER 2 \u2014 no single reading was correct alone.",
 "limits": "Reading 2's m23-24 were contaminated (it accidentally saw reading 1's checkpoint), so those bars are single-witness. The SPELLING E#3 in m21 follows from the printed sharp plus the C#7->F#m context; the sounding pitch is certain.",
 "why_it_scores_higher": "~79% here vs ~54% on the CC0 chamber set and ~56% on the Becker scale tables. That is not a better arranger, it is easier music: a beginner duet sits in first position with open strings, which is precisely the regime the model is built for and the regime the app teaches. Quote the three numbers together or none.",
